---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import AdSlot from '../components/AdSlot.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config/site.ts';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="What this site is, how it is built, and the rules it follows so that every number on it can be checked."
  path="/about/"
>
  <div class="container">
    <div class="page-head">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">Home</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
        <span>About</span>
      </nav>
      <h1>About {SITE.name}</h1>
      <p class="page-head__lede">
        {SITE.name} publishes financial calculators and the explanations behind
        them. It is a small site with a narrow purpose: produce a number you can
        check, and show you the working that produced it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What this site is</h2>
      <p>
        Three calculators, fifteen guides, and the primary sources behind both.
        The calculators take numbers you supply and apply a published formula:
        the amortization formula for a mortgage, a month-by-month cash
        comparison for renting against buying, and the compound interest
        formula for a savings balance.
      </p>
      <p>
        The site is funded by display advertising. That shapes how it is built.
        Pages load quickly and carry little JavaScript, because a page that
        loads fast keeps the reader long enough to see the advertising, and
        because a page that loads fast is the one people come back to. No
        calculator asks for an email address, and no result is gated.
      </p>

      <h2>What this site is not</h2>
      <p>
        It is not a brokerage, it does not accept applications, and it does not
        earn a commission from anyone who clicks anything. There are no
        sponsored placements, no affiliate links and no lead forms. The
        advertising is display advertising and nothing else, which means the
        only thing a reader's visit pays for is the reader's attention.
      </p>
      <p>
        No tool here evaluates your circumstances. A mortgage calculator that
        returned "put down 20 percent" would be wrong most of the time,
        because the right answer depends on things the page cannot see:
        your other savings, your job, your tolerance for a bad month. So every
        tool computes and stops there.
      </p>

      <h2>The rules the site follows</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Every tool states its limits.</strong> Under each result
          there is a block listing what the tool does not do, in the same words
          on every page.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Every figure that comes from somewhere has a source.</strong>
          Guides link to the primary source and carry the date the data was
          read. The site's own arithmetic is shown in full.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Every page carries a review date.</strong> A guide that has
          not been looked at in a long time says so.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>AI drafts, human review.</strong> Drafting tools help with
          the words. Every published page has been read by a person who
          checked the arithmetic, the units and the claims.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Who runs this</h2>
      <p>
        {SITE.author.name}. {SITE.author.bio} The
        <a href="/editorial-team/">editorial page</a> sets out what the review
        checks before a page goes live, and the
        <a href={SITE.repository} rel="noopener">code is public</a> — the
        formulas, the unit tests and the checks that block a release with a
        broken page.
      </p>

      <h2>How the calculators work</h2>
      <p>
        The arithmetic runs in your browser. There is no server that receives
        the numbers you type, and nothing about a calculation is sent to
        anyone. The formulas are implemented once, in one place, and covered
        by unit tests that check them against known published loan figures and
        against the invariants the mathematics requires — that a schedule
        sums to the amount borrowed, that a loan closes at exactly zero, that
        an interest-free balance grows by the deposits alone.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        If a figure is wrong, a source link is dead or a page contradicts
        itself, that is a defect and it should be fixed. The
        <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> says how to report one, and every
        correction results in a changed review date rather than a silent edit.
      </p>
    </div>

    <AdSlot placement="in-article" />

    <AdSlot placement="footer" />
  </div>
</Base>
